  "summary": "Gold cells filled with singing blues and reds — Georgia's answer to Byzantium's finest enamels.",
  "description": "Between the 9th and 13th centuries Georgian goldsmiths perfected minankari, cloisonné enamel icons and liturgical objects whose gold wire cells hold pools of luminous color. Masterworks like the Khakhuli triptych's enamel plaque and the Kintsvisi Monastery St. George rank with the finest Byzantine work anywhere, fusing Byzantine technique with Georgian saints, costumes and iconography. The Mongol invasions and empire collapse ended the medieval tradition; 20th-century revivalists reconstructed the craft, and Tbilisi workshops now fire gold-cell icons for churches and collectors, keeping one of the world's hardest jewel-crafts alive.",
  "culturalContext": "Minankari is Georgia's Golden Age in glass and gold: the Khakhuli triptych and Kintsvisi plaques expressed a kingdom claiming Byzantium's equals, and the 20th-century Tbilisi revival made enamel icons a national-craft institution — fired today for Orthodox churches, state gifts and the Georgian treasury's identity.",
